In Python, the terms alternative constructor and destructive constructor are often asked in interviews, but they mean different things.

🔹 1  Alternative Constructor
✅ What is an Alternative Constructor?
•	A class method used to create objects in different ways
•	Returns an instance of the class
•	Implemented using @classmethod
•	Does not replace __init__, it complements it
🔹 Why Use Alternative Constructor?
✔ Create object from different data formats
✔ Cleaner code
✔ Better readability
🔹 Example
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name, marks):
        self.name = name
        self.marks = marks

    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data):
        name, marks = data.split(",")
        return cls(name, int(marks))
s1 = Student("Anirudha", 90)
s2 = Student.from_string("Rahul,85")
🔹 Another Example (from file / dict)
@classmethod
def from_dict(cls, data):
    return cls(data["name"], data["marks"])
🔹 Key Points (Alternative Constructor)
✔ Uses @classmethod
✔ First argument is cls
✔ Returns cls(...)
✔ Used for multiple object creation styles


🔹 2  Destructive Constructor
⚠️ Important Truth
👉 Python does NOT have a real “destructive constructor”
What people call a destructive constructor usually refers to:
•	Destructor → __del__()
🔹 Destructor (__del__)
What is Destructor?
•	Called when an object is destroyed / garbage collected
•	Used to release resources
•	Not reliable for critical logic
🔹 Example
class Demo:
    def __init__(self):
        print("Object created")

    def __del__(self):
        print("Object destroyed")
d = Demo()
del d
📌 Output:
Object created
Object destroyed
🔹 Real-Life Use Case
•	Closing files
•	Releasing DB connections
•	Cleaning temporary resources
⚠️ Warning About Destructor
❌ Execution time is not guaranteed
❌ Avoid using it for important cleanup
✔ Use context managers (with) instead
🔹 Alternative vs Destructor (Comparison)
Feature	Alternative Constructor	Destructor
Purpose	Create object differently	Destroy object
Decorator	@classmethod	None
Method name	Any name	__del__
Returns object	✅ Yes	❌ No
Reliability	High	Low

🔹 Interview Tip 
👉 If asked “destructive constructor”, clarify politely:
Python doesn’t have a destructive constructor; it has a destructor (__del__).

